# The spline tour: follow a FITTED PATH with pure pursuit.
#
# This is the only tour here that is not a chain of arcs the motion
# engine shapes on its own. The path is a densely sampled curve
# (complex.path.json -- 321 points, 3493.6 mm long, imported from an
# SVG and fitted to the field), and the host closes a loop around the
# robot's odometry pose to follow it:
#
#   1. read the pose off the telemetry stream
#   2. slide the arc-length cursor to the nearest point on the path
#   3. take the point one LOOKAHEAD further along -- the point where
#      the pursuit circle leaves the path ahead of the robot
#   4. turn the offset to that point into a curvature and command it
#   5. repeat, every INTERVAL seconds
#
# Curvature from the standard pure-pursuit geometry: with the aim point
# at (dx, dy) in the robot frame and L = |aim|, kappa = 2*dy / L^2, and
# omega = kappa * v. Commanded with MOVE_V (body speed + yaw rate held
# for a duration) so the robot keeps rolling between updates. Discrete
# MOVE_X hops to each aim point would also "drive at the point over and
# over", but would stop and re-accelerate every cycle -- a stutter
# rather than a followed curve.
#
# WHAT IT MEASURES that the arc tours cannot: cross-track error against
# a reference curve, continuously. Every other tour scores the endpoint
# (closure) and infers the middle. This scores the middle.
#
# LOOKAHEAD IS THE TUNING KNOB and it trades two errors against each
# other. Too short and the follower oscillates -- it steers hard at a
# point it reaches before the robot can respond. Too long and it cuts
# corners: it cannot track curvature much tighter than about half the
# lookahead. complex.path.json's min_radius_mm is 32.0, which is
# tighter than this robot tracks at any speed, so SOME corner-cutting
# is expected on the sharpest bends and is a property of the path, not
# a fault in the follower.
#
# SIZING. The path spans +-360.9 x +-262.8 mm, well inside the working
# budget of +-550 x +-350 (tours/FIELD.md), with 189 mm spare on x and
# 87 mm on y. It is used UNSCALED on purpose: scaling would change
# min_radius_mm and break comparison with radio-robot-elite's own runs
# of the same path. Stage the robot at the field centre; the path is
# closed and centred on its own origin.

# PIVOT TUNING (MEASURED gopiv 2026-09-01, interleaved A/B so battery
# drift hits both arms equally). Pivot cruise 188 mm/s (omega 180 deg/s)
# beats 300 mm/s (286 deg/s) decisively: excess +1.34 deg sd 0.35 versus
# +6.88 deg sd 1.03, n=8 each. pivot_overrun was then swept AT 188 mm/s
# and 0.68 -- the value the closure sweep found at 300 mm/s -- leaves
# +1.44 deg on the table; 2.0 is the zero crossing (1.8 -> +0.29,
# 2.2 -> -0.25). pivot_overrun is speed-dependent: retune it whenever
# the pivot cruise changes.

SET twist_hold_gain 8
SET speed_floor 512
SET yaw_taper 800
SET pivot_overrun 2.0

MARK spline
SPLINE file=complex.path.json speed=200 lookahead=160 laps=1 interval=0.12

STOP dwell=0.5
